<!--{ sw00 : formula | hidden }-->
<% texl.import "~/lib/tutorial/index.texl" ~> (lib -> bind "tutorial" lib.data) %>
<!--{ sw01 : rich }-->
# 13. The formula language

*Advanced.* Formulas are written in **Swan**, the language of Texl, the
format of Stilo's documents. Swan has lists, namespaces and functions,
enough to write a whole calculation. This lesson shows the three you need
most. About eight minutes.
<!--{ sw02 : markdown }-->
## Lists and namespaces

A **list** holds values in order, between square brackets; a **namespace**
holds named values, between braces:

- `spans = [4, 5, 6]` is a list: `spans 0` is its first item, <% spans = [4, 5, 6] %><% spans 0 %>,
  and `list.size spans` is <% list.size spans %>.
- `beam = {span: 6, load: 12.5}` is a namespace: `beam.span` is
  <% beam = {span: 6, load: 12.5} %><% beam.span %>.

The steel library of lesson 8 is a namespace of namespaces, and a data table
(lesson 7) is a namespace with the members `columns`, `rows`, `column` and
`row`.
<!--{ sw03 : markdown }-->
## Functions

A **function** turns values into a value. It is written with an arrow: its
parameters on the left, the result on the right. Then you call it with its
values after its name, as you call `math.sqrt 16`:

<% tutorial.tag "area = (b, h) -> b * h" %> gives `area (3, 4)`, which is
<% area = (b, h) -> b * h %><% area (3, 4) %>.

**Your turn.** On the empty line just above the check below, press **=**
and write a function `moment` of a load `q` and a span `L`:
`moment = (q, L) -> q * L ^ 2 / 8`.
<!--{ sw04 : rich }-->

<!--{ sw05 : formula }-->
<% tutorial.check (moment (10, 4) == 20) %>
<!--{ sw06 : markdown }-->
## Conditions

`condition ? a ; b` gives `a` if the condition is true, and `b` otherwise
(lesson 4). In a function, it decides for any value you give it.

**Your turn.** On the empty line just above the check below, write a
function `verdict` that gives the text `"OK"` for a utilisation `u` up to 1,
and `"not OK"` above it: `verdict = u -> u <= 1 ? "OK" ; "not OK"`.
<!--{ sw07 : rich }-->

<!--{ sw08 : formula }-->
<% tutorial.check ((verdict 0.8 == "OK") & (verdict 1.2 == "not OK")) %>
<!--{ sw09 : markdown }-->
## A function for every item

`=>` applies a function to each of several values. With `list.enum`, which
turns a list into separate values, and square brackets around the result, it
makes a new list from an old one.

**Your turn.** On the empty line just above the check below, write
`moments = [(list.enum spans) => (L -> moment (10, L))]`: the moments of a
load of 10 kN/m on each span of `spans`.
<!--{ sw10 : rich }-->

<!--{ sw11 : formula }-->
<% tutorial.check (moments == [20, 31.25, 45]) %>
<!--{ sw12 : markdown }-->
The moments are <% list.enum moments ; "…" %> kNm.

The manual describes the whole language, with every function it has:
[The Swan language](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/swan/index.html),
[Functions](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/swan/functions.html),
[Lists, text and namespaces](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/swan/collections.html)
and the [built-in library](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/swan/builtins/index.html).
<!--{ sw13 : rich }-->
